I need to extend the existing MVEL2 rule derivation implementation to support **derived attributes**, while keeping the implementation simple, maintainable, and backward compatible.

## Current behavior

Currently, business rules support only attributes that are directly available in the request payload/context.

Example:

```text
(heder[pymtStts] == 'SUC' || heder[pymtStts] == 'ACT')
&& data.amt[dbtCcr] == 'SGD'
```

The rule is compiled and then evaluated against the request payload/context.

## New requirement

We now need to support **derived attributes**.

Example derived attribute:

```text
Name:
RECSTATUS

Expression:
(heder[pymtStts] == 'SUC' || heder[pymtStts] == 'ACT')
```

A business rule can then use:

```text
RECSTATUS == true && data.amt[dbtCcr] == 'SGD'
```

## Important design decision

Do NOT modify, expand, or replace the business-rule expression during compilation.

For example, do NOT transform:

```text
RECSTATUS == true && data.amt[dbtCcr] == 'SGD'
```

into:

```text
(heder[pymtStts] == 'SUC' || heder[pymtStts] == 'ACT')
&& data.amt[dbtCcr] == 'SGD'
```

The business rule should remain unchanged and should be compiled normally by MVEL2.

Instead, during rule compilation/configuration processing, determine whether the business rule references any configured derived attributes.

For example:

```text
Business rule:
RECSTATUS == true && data.amt[dbtCcr] == 'SGD'

Compiled metadata:
requiredDerivedAttributes = [RECSTATUS]
```

If a rule does not reference any derived attributes:

```text
Business rule:
ACCOUNT_TYPE == 'N' && data.amt[dbtCcr] == 'USD'

Compiled metadata:
requiredDerivedAttributes = []
```

## Runtime behavior

At runtime, evaluate only the derived attributes required by the particular rule.

The desired flow is:

```text
Request
  ↓
Build evaluation context
  ↓
Get compiled rule
  ↓
Check required derived attributes
  ↓
For each required derived attribute:
      Is it already available in context?
          YES → reuse existing value
          NO  → evaluate its compiled MVEL2 expression
                 ↓
                 add result to context
  ↓
Evaluate the original compiled business rule
  ↓
Return result
```

Example:

Initial context:

```text
heder[pymtStts] = "SUC"
data.amt[dbtCcr] = "SGD"
```

Rule metadata:

```text
requiredDerivedAttributes = [RECSTATUS]
```

`RECSTATUS` is not currently in the context, so evaluate:

```text
heder[pymtStts] == 'SUC' || heder[pymtStts] == 'ACT'
```

Result:

```text
RECSTATUS = true
```

Add it to the evaluation context:

```text
heder[pymtStts] = "SUC"
data.amt[dbtCcr] = "SGD"
RECSTATUS = true
```

Then evaluate the original compiled rule:

```text
RECSTATUS == true && data.amt[dbtCcr] == 'SGD'
```

## Lazy evaluation is important

Do NOT calculate all configured derived attributes for every request.

A derived attribute may be used by only one rule.

For example:

```text
Rule R001
Derived attributes:
    RECSTATUS

Rule R002
Derived attributes:
    none

Rule R003
Derived attributes:
    DATRSEPABENEBICVAL
```

When evaluating R002, do not calculate `RECSTATUS` or `DATRSEPABENEBICVAL`.

Only calculate derived attributes that the current rule actually requires.

## Reuse existing context values

Before evaluating a derived attribute, check whether the value already exists in the evaluation context.

Conceptually:

```java
if (!context.containsKey(attributeName)) {
    Object value = MVEL.executeExpression(
        derivedAttribute.getCompiledExpression(),
        context
    );

    context.put(attributeName, value);
}
```

This prevents duplicate calculation.

Do not modify the original request DTO unless the existing architecture specifically requires it. Prefer enriching the internal MVEL2 evaluation context.

## Compilation / metadata

Please inspect the existing implementation and determine the best place to add metadata to the compiled rule.

The compiled rule should conceptually contain:

```java
CompiledRule {
    Serializable compiledBusinessExpression;

    List<CompiledDerivedAttribute> requiredDerivedAttributes;
}
```

And a derived attribute should conceptually contain:

```java
CompiledDerivedAttribute {
    String name;

    Serializable compiledExpression;
}
```

Derived expressions must be compiled once when the rule/configuration is loaded or reloaded, not for every transaction.

## Identifying derived attributes used by a rule

At compile/configuration time, determine which configured derived attributes are referenced by the business rule.

For example, if the configured derived attributes are:

```text
RECSTATUS
DATRSEPABENEBICVAL
CUSTOMER_ELIGIBLE
```

and the rule is:

```text
RECSTATUS == true
&& data.amt[dbtCcr] == 'SGD'
```

then the compiled rule metadata should contain:

```text
requiredDerivedAttributes = [RECSTATUS]
```

Do not simply perform an unsafe substring replacement of the rule expression.

Use the existing MVEL2 capabilities or the safest approach that fits the current project architecture to identify references.

If the existing codebase already has a mechanism for parsing/tokenizing rule attributes, reuse it.

## Derived attribute configuration

Support a model similar to:

```java
DerivedAttributeDefinition {
    String name;
    String expression;
}
```

Example:

```text
name = RECSTATUS

expression =
(heder[pymtStts] == 'SUC' || heder[pymtStts] == 'ACT')
```

The design should also support future derived attributes such as:

```text
DATRSEPABENEBICVAL
CUSTOMER_ELIGIBLE
...
```

## Important: don't over-engineer

Do not create a completely new rule engine.

Reuse:

* existing MVEL2 compilation
* existing MVEL2 evaluation
* existing rule loading/reloading
* existing rule caching
* existing evaluation context
* existing configuration structures where practical

The goal is to add a small derived-attribute capability around the existing rule engine.

## Backward compatibility

Existing rules must continue to work without any changes.

For example:

```text
heder[pymtStts] == 'SUC'
&& data.amt[dbtCcr] == 'SGD'
```

should continue to be compiled and evaluated exactly as before.

Rules with no derived attributes should have:

```text
requiredDerivedAttributes = []
```

and should not perform any derived-attribute processing.

## Example final implementation behavior

Conceptually:

```java
public boolean evaluate(
        CompiledRule rule,
        Map<String, Object> context) {

    for (CompiledDerivedAttribute derived :
            rule.getRequiredDerivedAttributes()) {

        if (!context.containsKey(derived.getName())) {

            Object value = MVEL.executeExpression(
                    derived.getCompiledExpression(),
                    context
            );

            context.put(derived.getName(), value);
        }
    }

    Object result = MVEL.executeExpression(
            rule.getCompiledBusinessExpression(),
            context
    );

    return Boolean.TRUE.equals(result);
}
```

Please adapt this to the existing project's classes and coding conventions rather than blindly creating new classes.

